1. Critical: MSP path vs exception return (pendsv.rs)
When the interrupted thread used MSP (CONTROL.SPSEL == 0), the hardware pushes the eight-word frame onto MSP. The handler then does adds r0, msp, hw_frame_bytes / msr msp, r0 to skip that frame, merges into .L_pendsv_restore, sets PSP for the new task, and executes bx r14.

On entry, LR is EXC_RETURN. For a thread that was using MSP that is 0xFFFFFFF9 (return to Thread mode, continue using MSP for unstacking). The return sequence therefore unstacks eight words from MSP into the core registers. You have already moved MSP past the real frame, so the CPU loads the wrong eight words from MSP. That is undefined behaviour relative to the intended context.

If the intent is to abandon the MSP-based thread and start (or resume) a PSP task, you must end with an EXC_RETURN that selects PSP (typically 0xFFFFFFFD for non‑FPU), or an equivalent pattern used in ports like FreeRTOS for the “first switch from MSP”. Your own comments reference those constants but the MSP path never applies them.

The PSP path does not have this problem if LR already contains 0xFFFFFFFD: the hardware then unstacks from PSP, which you just set to the new task’s frame.


